WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:20.000
Let's talk about what this program is going to do for you.

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:40.000
The HVAC industry offers four career levels: Apprentice (0-2 years, $16-22/hr),
Journeyman (2-5 years, $22-32/hr), Master Technician (5-10 years, $32-45/hr),
and Contractor/Business Owner ($50-100K+ annually). This lesson maps each level

00:00:40.000 --> 00:01:00.000
Here are the key terms. Write these down.

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:20.000
Journeyman: Licensed technician who has completed required OJT hours (typically
2,000+) and passed the state exam. Can work independently.

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:40.000
Master Technician: Experienced technician with advanced certifications (NATE,
manufacturer-specific) who can diagnose complex system problems.

00:01:40.000 --> 00:02:00.000
NATE: North American Technician Excellence — the leading third-party
certification for HVAC technicians, recognized by all major manufacturers.

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:20.000
Here's why this matters on the job: Knowing the career ladder helps you
negotiate your first job. An employer offering $16/hr with a clear path to
journeyman is often better than $18/hr with no advancement plan.

00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:40.000
Watch out for these: Track your OJT hours from day one — you will need
documentation for licensure. And — Ask potential employers about their
apprenticeship structure during interviews

00:02:40.000 --> 00:03:00.000
Take a minute before you move on. What's the one thing from this lesson you need
to remember on a service call?

